    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d897">

                <pb facs="1073" n="1063" sameAs="frus:frus1943v01/pg1063"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">800.515/568: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Algiers</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Wiley</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Algiers</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1943-03-30T15:00:00+01:00">March 30, 1943—3
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received March 31—12:05 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>515. From Murphy. Your 477, March 17, 11 p.m. Careful consideration has been
                    given to the question of sending a French expert to Washington to participate in
                    exploratory conversations regarding monetary stabilization in postwar period.
                    The best qualified person whom the French would like to designate is Maurice
                    Couve de Murville.<anchor n="23" xml:id="d897-f1"/> Other possibilities are
                    Hervé Alphand<anchor n="24" xml:id="d897-f2"/> and Leroy-Beaulieu,<anchor n="25"
                        xml:id="d897-f3"/> but preference is decidedly in favor of Couve de
                    Murville. However, his functions as Secretary General would not permit his <pb
                        facs="1074" n="1064" xml:id="pg1064"/> departure at the present time. I feel
                    that unless this question is urgent, a decision should be deferred. What will be
                    the probable date of these discussions? [Murphy.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Wiley</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
